\section{Introduction}
The purpose of this note is to describe a small modal type theory with internalized parametricity.
Internalized parametricity means we should have {\em internal} witnesses of ``free theorems''. For example,
if we have a polymorphic function
\[ q : (X : \rtype) \to X \to X \]
then there should be a proof, in the type theory itself, that $q$ is the identity function.

There are lots of other type theories for internalized parametricity.
This might even be a special case of one of the existing ones; I'm not
sure. Some constraints that I am intentionally putting on this one:
\begin{itemize}
\item It avoids an explicit interval type.
\item It does not require complex composition principles for computation.
\item It does not handle iterated internalized parametricity.
\item It does not definitionally {\em impose} the meaning of parametricity for each type former.
Parametricity properties are instead derivable. For example,
there will turn out to be a derivation in the type theory that says, in effect,
``a function is `in the relation' if it always maps related inputs to related outputs''.
\item The syntax is split into two {\em levels}, called $\wid{\hbox{wide}}$ and $\nar{\hbox{narrow}}$.
Wide things are things `equipped with parametricity data', and narrow things are those without.
From a category theoretic point of view, if the category of narrow expressions is $\C$, the category
of wide expressions is the arrow category $\C^{\bullet \to \bullet}$. More directly, we can say that a wide type is nothing more
than a narrow type `at the base' with another narrow type `lying above it`. This is really just a function between pair of narrow types,
but the ``fibered picture'' is a useful perspective.
\item The theory is modal. Hypotheses in the context may exist at various modes.
\item There are two novel type constructors compared to normal
  dependent type theory, named (drawing clear inspiration from other type
  systems) `glue types' and `fiber types'.
\end{itemize}


\section{Language}


%% The type theory is modal and has two levels, called $\wid{\hbox{wide}}$ and $\nar{\hbox{narrow}}$.

%% One pervasive intuition that is helpful to lean on is that we are, in this theory,
%% ``programming in the presheaf category $\rtype^{\bf 2}$'', where $\bf 2$ is the category
%% with two objects and one trivial morphism.


In both levels, we have several sorts of variables in the context.
Variables may be ordinary $x : \wid A$, strong $x :: \wid A$, or weak $x \div \nar A$.
Ordinary variables may only appear in wide contexts.


\[\begin{tabular}{rccl}
 Wide Contexts&$\wid \Gamma$&$::=$&$\cdot  \celse \wid\Gamma, x :: \wid A \celse \wid\Gamma, x \div \nar A  \celse \wid \Gamma, x : \wid A$\\
  Narrow Contexts&$\nar\Gamma$&$::=$&$\cdot \celse \nar \Gamma, x :: \wid A\celse \nar \Gamma, x \div \nar A  $\\
  Wide Types&$\wid A$&$::=$&$\cdots
 \celse  (x : \wid A) \to \wid B
 \celse \gg (x : \nar A) . \nar B
$\\
  Narrow Types&$\nar A$&$::=$&$\cdots
 \celse  (x : \nar A) \to \nar B
  \celse \pp {\nar M} {\wid A}
$\\
Wide  Terms&$\wid M$&$::=$&$\cdots
\celse \lambda x . \wid M \celse \wid M\ \wid N
\celse \epair{\nar M }{\nar N}
\celse \bglue(\nar M, \nar N)
$\\
Narrow  Terms&$\nar M$&$::=$&$\cdots
\celse \lambda x . \nar M \celse \nar M\ \nar N
\celse \{ \wid M \}
\celse \bunglue(\wid M)
$\\
\end{tabular}\]

Every type constructor that we'd expect to exist in ordinary dependent type theory will exist
in both narrow and wide types. For example, I've included $\Pi$ types above.

Glue types $ \gg (x : \nar A) . \nar B$ are a way of building a wide
type out of two narrow types $\nar A$ and $\nar B$. The variable $x$
is bound in $\nar B$. See Figure~\ref{fig:glue}. The introduction and elimination forms
for glue types are $\bglue(\nar M, \nar N)$ and $\bunglue(\wid M)$.

Fiber types $ \pp {\nar M} {\wid A}$ are a way
of extracting the `fiber above $\nar M$' from the wide type $\wid A$. See Figure~\ref{fig:fiber}.
The introduction and elimination forms
for glue types are $\{\wid M\}$ and $\epair{\nar M }{\nar N}$.


