To obtain the base type of a wide type $\wid A$, we use {\em erasure} to obtain the narrow type $|\wid A|$, which is
described immediately below.

\section{Syntactic Operations}
\subsection{Erasure}
Erasure $|\dash|$ takes a wide type or term and turns it into a narrow type or term, by
discarding ``all the upstairs information''. This is a simple homomorphism on the usual dependent type theory syntax.
On glue types, glue introduction, fiber elimination, we have two pieces of information and we keep the first (`downstairs')
and throw away the second (`upstairs').

\[
\begin{tabular}{rcl@{\qquad}l}
$\erase{(x : \wid A) \to \wid B}$&$=$&$(x : \erase{\wid A}) \to \erase{\wid B}$\\
$\erase{\lambda x.\wid M}$&$=$&$\lambda x.\erase{\wid M}$\\
 $\erase{\wid M\ \wid N} $&$=$&$ \erase{\wid M}\ \erase{\wid N}$\\
$\erase x$&$=$&$x$ \\
$\erase{\gg(x : \nar A).\nar B}$&$=$&$\nar A$\\
$\erase{\bglue(\nar M, \nar N)}$&$=$&$\nar M$\\
$\erase{\epair{\nar M}{\nar N}}$&$=$&$\nar M$\\
$\erase{\wid\rtype}$&$=$&$\nar \rtype$\\
\end{tabular}
\]




\subsection{Promotion and Demotion}

The typing rules will require some operations on contexts.
We define operations $\promote\dash$ and $\demote\dash$, both of which take a wide context to a narrow context.
Demotion turns normal variables into weak variables and leaves other variables alone.
Promotion turns normal variables into strong variables and leaves other variables alone.

\[\begin{tabular}{rcl}
 $ \promote{(\wid\Gamma, x : \wid A)}$&$:=$&$(\promote{\wid\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)$\\
 $ \promote{(\wid\Gamma, x :: \wid A)}$&$:=$&$(\promote{\wid\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)$\\
 $ \promote{(\wid\Gamma, x \div \nar A)}$&$:=$&$(\promote{\wid\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)$\\
 $ \promote{(\cdot)}$&$:=$&$\cdot$\\
\end{tabular}
\qquad
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
 $ \demote{(\wid\Gamma, x : \wid A)}$&$:=$&$(\demote{\wid\Gamma}, x \div \erase{\wid A})$\\
 $ \demote{(\wid\Gamma, x :: \wid A)}$&$:=$&$(\demote{\wid\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)$\\
 $ \demote{(\wid\Gamma, x \div \nar A)}$&$:=$&$(\demote{\wid\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)$\\
 $ \demote{(\cdot)}$&$:=$&$\cdot$\\
\end{tabular}\]


\subsection{Inclusion}
Narrow contexts are a subset of wide contexts. We write $\ups$ for the explicit inclusion of narrow contexts into wide contexts.
\[
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
 $ \ups(\nar\Gamma, x :: \wid A)$&$:=$&$(\ups\nar\Gamma, x :: \wid A)$\\
 $ \ups(\nar\Gamma, x \div \nar A)$&$:=$&$(\ups\nar\Gamma, x \div \nar A)$\\
 $ \ups(\cdot)$&$:=$&$\cdot$\\
\end{tabular}\]

As abbreviations, we define
\[\rdemote {\wid A} = \ups(\demote {\wid A}) \qquad \rpromote {\wid A} = \ups(\promote {\wid A})\]

\section{Typing Rules}

Now we have all the tools we need to give typing rules for the system.
\subsection{Context Formation}
Each type of variable goes with some operation on the context in which its type must be well-formed.
At this point in the narrative it admittedly seems unclear why these operations are the right ones.
We give some intuition below in Section~\ref{todo}.
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\wprov \cdot \rctx
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\nprov \cdot \rctx
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wprov {\wid\Gamma} \rctx \qquad {\wid\Gamma} \wprov \wid A : \rtype
\justifies
\wprov ({\wid\Gamma}, x : \wid A)\rctx
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wprov {\wid\Gamma} \rctx \qquad \demote {\wid \Gamma} \nprov \nar A : \rtype
\justifies
\wprov ({\wid\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)\rctx
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wprov {\wid\Gamma} \rctx \qquad \rdemote{\wid\Gamma} \wprov \wid A : \rtype
\justifies
\wprov ({\wid\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)\rctx
\end{prooftree}
\]


\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nprov {\nar\Gamma} \rctx \qquad {\nar\Gamma} \nprov \nar A : \rtype
\justifies
\nprov ({\nar\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)\rctx
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\nprov {\nar\Gamma} \rctx \qquad \ups {\nar\Gamma} \wprov \wid A : \rtype
\justifies
\nprov ({\nar\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)\rctx
\end{prooftree}
\]

\subsection{Variable Rules}
Most variables can be immediately used, with the exception of weak variables in wide contexts.
This is the sense in which weak variables are weak.
By constrast, it appears from these rules alone that there is relatively little difference between ordinary
variables and strong variables: a strong variable at type $\wid A$ can be used, to produce a term of type $\wid A$,
and so can an ordinary variable. The difference between them as hypotheses comes from how they interact with demotion.
An ordinary variable becomes weak under demotion, whereas a strong variable is resilient to demotion and stays strong.

The one remaining phenomenon of note is that when a strong variable is used in a weak context, then the type must be
erased down to a narrow type.
\[
\begin{prooftree}
x : \wid A \in  \wid\Gamma
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov x : \wid A
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Gamma
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov x : \wid A
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
x \div \nar A \in  \nar\Gamma
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov x : \nar A
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Gamma
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov x :  \erase {\wid A}
\end{prooftree}
\]

\subsection{The Fiber Type}
Here are the formation, introduction, and elimination rules for the fiber type.

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\ups\nar \Gamma \wprov \wid A : \wid\rtype \quad \nar \Gamma \nprov \nar M : \erase {\wid A}
\justifies
\nar \Gamma \nprov \pp {\nar M} {\wid A} : \nar\rtype
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\ups {\nar \Gamma} \wprov \wid M : \wid A
\justifies
\nar \Gamma \nprov \{\wid M\} : \pp  {\erase{\wid M}} {\wid A}
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Gamma} \nprov \nar M : \erase{\wid A} \quad
\promote{\wid\Gamma} \nprov \nar N : \pp{\nar M}{\wid A}
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov \epair{\nar M}{\nar N} : \wid A
\end{prooftree}
\]
In the elimination rule, $\wid A$ is a type over $\wid\Gamma$; the second premise
is well-formed because we can transport $\wid A$ to $\rpromote{\wid\Gamma}$ by
Lemma~\ref{promotion.weakening} and transport $\nar M$ to $\promote{\wid\Gamma}$ by Lemma~\ref{narrow.weakening}.
% XXX this forward reference is a bit too much. Decide what to do about it.

We have a $\beta$ principle for definitional equality
\[
\epair{\nar M}{\{\wid N\}} \equiv_\beta \wid N
\]
If $\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar N : \pp{\nar M}{\wid A}$ then we have an $\eta$ definitional equality
\[
  \nar N \equiv_\eta \{\epair{\nar M}{\nar N}\}
\]



\subsection{The Glue Type}
Here are the formation, introduction, and elimination rules for the glue type.

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Gamma} \nprov \nar A : \nar\rtype \qquad
\promote{\wid\Gamma}, x \div \nar A \nprov \nar B : \nar\rtype
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B : \wid\rtype
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Gamma} \nprov \nar M : \nar A \qquad
\promote{\wid\Gamma} \nprov \nar N : \nsub{\nar M/_{\div}x}\nar B
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov \bglue(\nar M, \nar N) : \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\ups\nar\Gamma \wprov \wid M : \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov \bunglue \wid M : \nsub{\erase{\wid M}/_{\div}x}\nar B
\end{prooftree}
\]
See below in Section~\ref{sec.substitution} for the exact meaning of the substitution operation used in the introduction and elimination rules.
We have a $\beta$ principle for definitional equality
\[
\bunglue(\bglue(\nar M,\nar N)) \equiv_\beta \nar N
\]
If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid M : \gg(x : \nar A) . \nar B$
 then we have an $\eta$ definitional equality
\[
 \wid M \equiv_\eta \bglue(\erase{\wid M}, \bunglue \wid M)
\]

\subsection{Other Type Constructors}
Other type constructors work `as you would expect' but we must take note of what happens to binders.
The rule is that at wide types, we introduce ordinary hypotheses, and at narrow types, we introduce weak hypotheses.
For example, here are the introduction and elimination rules for $\Pi$ types.

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\Gamma, x \div \nar A \nprov \nar B
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov (x : \nar A) \to \nar B
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\Gamma, x : \wid A \wprov \wid B
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov (x : \wid A) \to \wid B
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\Gamma, x \div \nar A\nprov \nar M : \nar B
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov \lambda x . \nar M : (x : \nar A) \to \nar B
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\Gamma, x : \wid A\wprov \wid M : \wid B
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov \lambda x . \wid M : (x : \wid A) \to \wid B
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\Gamma \nprov \nar M : (x : \nar A) \to \nar B \qquad
\nar\Gamma \nprov \nar N : \nar A
\justifies
\nar\Gamma \nprov \nar M\ \nar N : \nsub{\nar N/_\div x}\nar B
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\Gamma \wprov \wid M : (x : \wid A) \to \wid B \qquad
\wid\Gamma \wprov \wid N : \wid A
\justifies
\wid\Gamma \wprov \wid M\ \wid N : \wsub{\wid N/x}\wid B
\end{prooftree}
\]

\section{Substitution}

\subsection{Definition of Substitution}
We have syntactic forms for simultaneous substitutions at both the wide and narrow levels.

\[\begin{tabular}{rccl}
 Wide Substitutions&$\wid \theta$&$::=$&$\cdot
\celse \wid\theta\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x}
\celse \wid\theta\wsub{\nar M /_{\div} x}
\celse \wid\theta\wsub{\wid M / x}
$\\
 Narrow Substitutions&$\nar \theta$&$::=$&$\cdot
\celse \nar\theta\nsub{\wid M /_{::} x}
\celse \nar\theta\nsub{\nar M /_{\div} x}
$\\
\end{tabular}\]
We have two typing judgments
\[\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta : \wid \Delta\qquad \nar \Gamma \nprov \nar\theta : \nar \Delta\]
 which are defined by

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta : \wid \Delta \qquad \wid \Gamma \wprov \wid M :  \wid\theta \wid A
\justifies
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta \wsub{\wid M / x} : (\wid \Delta, x : \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta : \wid \Delta \qquad \rdemote{\wid \Gamma} \wprov \wid M : \rdemote{\wid\theta} \wid A
\justifies
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x} : (\wid \Delta, x :: \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta : \wid \Delta \qquad \demote{\wid \Gamma} \nprov \nar M : \demote{\wid\theta} \nar A
\justifies
\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid\theta\wsub{\nar M /_{\div} x} : (\wid \Delta, x \div \nar A)
\end{prooftree}
\]

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar\theta : \nar \Delta \qquad \ups{\nar \Gamma} \wprov \wid M : \ups{\nar\theta} \wid A
\justifies
\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar\theta\nsub{\wid M /_{::} x} : (\nar \Delta, x :: \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar\theta : \nar \Delta \qquad {\nar \Gamma} \nprov \nar M : {\nar\theta} \nar A
\justifies
\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar\theta\nsub{\nar M /_{\div} x} : (\nar \Delta, x \div \nar A)
\end{prooftree}
\]

Definition of demotion, promotion, and inclusion on substitutions:
We define the operations $\demote\dash$, $\promote\dash$, and $\ups\dash$ on substitutions by
\[\begin{array}{rcl}
  \demote{ (\wid\theta\wsub{\wid M/x})} &=& \demote{\wid\theta} \nsub{\erase{\wid M}/_{\div} x}\\
  \demote{(\wid\theta\wsub{\nar M/_{\div} x})} &=& \demote{\wid\theta}\nsub{{\nar M}/_{\div} x}\\
  \demote{(\wid\theta\wsub{\wid M/_{::} x})} &=& \demote{\wid\theta} \nsub{{\wid M}/_{::} x}\\
\end{array}
\qquad
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \promote{(\wid\theta\wsub{\wid M/x})} &=& \promote{\wid\theta} \nsub{{\wid M}/_{::} x}\\
  \promote{(\wid\theta\wsub{\nar M/_{\div} x})} &=& \promote{\wid\theta} \nsub{{\nar M}/_{\div} x}\\
  \promote{(\wid\theta\wsub{\wid M/_{::} x})} &=& \promote{\wid\theta} \nsub{{\wid M}/_{::} x}\\
\end{array}
\]
\[
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \ups(\nar\theta\nsub{\nar M/_{\div} x}) &=& (\ups\nar\theta) \wsub{{\nar M}/_{\div} x}\\
  \ups(\nar\theta\nsub{\wid M/_{::} x}) &=& (\ups\nar\theta) \wsub{{\wid M}/_{::} x}\\
\end{array}
\]

The definition of substitution as an operation is by mutual recursion over wide and narrow expressions.
On variables, substitution follows the variable typing rules. Specifically, a weak hypothesis cannot be used in
a wide expression, so it is an invariant that $\wsub{\nar M/_{\div}x}$ is never applied to the variable $x$ in a well-typed
term. Substitution for a strong variable used in a narrow expression results in an erased term. The definition of substitution on variables is:
\[
\begin{tabular}{rcll}
$\wid\theta(x)$&$=$&$\wid M$&if $\wsub{\wid M/x} \in \wid\theta$\\
$\wid\theta(x)$&$=$&$\wid M$&if $\wsub{\wid M/_{::}x} \in \wid\theta$\\
$\nar\theta(x)$&$=$&$\nar M$&if $\nsub{\nar M/_{\div} x} \in \nar \theta$\\
$\nar\theta(x)$&$=$&$\erase{\wid M}$&if $\nsub{\wid M/_{::}x} \in \nar\theta$\\
$\wid\theta (x)$&$=$&$x$&if $x \not\in \wid\theta$\\
$\nar\theta (x)$&$=$&$x$&if $x \not\in \nar\theta$\\
\end{tabular}
\]

Substitution on ordinary dependent type theory expressions are structural.
\[
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
$\wid\theta((y : \wid A) \to \wid B)$&$=$&$(y : \wid\theta\wid A) \to \wid\theta\wid B$\\
$\wid\theta(\lambda y . \wid M)$&$=$&$\lambda y . \wid\theta\wid M$\\
$\wid\theta(\wid M\ \wid N)$&$=$&$(\wid\theta\wid M)\ (\wid\theta\wid N)$\\
$\wid\theta(\wid\rtype)$&$=$&$\wid\rtype$\\
\end{tabular}
\qquad
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
$\nar\theta((y : \nar A) \to \nar B)$&$=$&$(y : \nar\theta\nar A) \to \nar\theta\nar B$\\
$\nar\theta(\lambda y . \nar M)$&$=$&$\lambda y . \nar\theta\nar M$\\
$\nar\theta(\nar M\ \nar N)$&$=$&$(\nar\theta\nar M)\ (\nar\theta\nar N)$\\
$\nar\theta(\nar\rtype)$&$=$&$\nar\rtype$\\
\end{tabular}
\]

The remaining expressions pass between levels, and follow the premises of the rule that introduces the expression.
When a subexpression lives, according to the typing rules, in a demoted (resp. promoted, included) context, the corresponding
 substitution is demoted (resp. promoted, included) as well.
TODO
and then define substitution on level-crossing expressions by
\[
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
$\wid\theta(\gg(y : \nar A).\nar B)$&$=$&$\gg(y : \demote{\wid\theta}\nar A).\promote{\wid\theta}\nar B$\\
$\wid\theta(\bglue(\nar M, \nar N))$&$=$&$\bglue({\demote{\wid\theta}}\nar M, {\promote{\wid\theta}}\nar N)$\\
$\wid\theta(\epair{\nar M}{\nar N})$&$=$&$\epair{{\demote{\wid\theta}}\nar M}{{\promote{\wid\theta}}\nar N}$\\
\end{tabular}
\qquad
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
$\nar\theta\pp{\nar M}{\wid A}$&$=$&$\pp{\nar\theta\nar M}{(\ups{\nar\theta})\wid A}$\\
$\nar\theta\{\wid M\}$&$=$&$\{(\ups{\nar\theta})\wid M\}$\\
$\nar\theta(\bunglue \wid M)$&$=$&$\bunglue((\ups{\nar\theta})\wid M)$\\
\end{tabular}
\]

Substitution is capture-avoiding. In a clause such as $\nar\theta(\lambda y . \nar M) = \lambda y . \nar\theta\nar M$,
the bound variable $y$ is assumed fresh for $\nar\theta$, so that $\nar\theta$ acts as the identity on it.
Equivalently, the right-hand side could be written $\lambda y . (\nar\theta\nsub{y/_\div y})\nar M$.
We use the latter notation when the typing of the extended substitution is what is at issue.

\subsection{Weakening}
We define a weakening relation $\Gamma' \le \Gamma$ on contexts that says $\Gamma$ `can be weakened to' $\Gamma'$.
This is ordered so that when we state the weakening lemma it looks like cutting out a $\Gamma$ in the middle:
``If $\Gamma' \le \Gamma$ and $\Gamma \prov J$, then $\Gamma' \prov J$''.

We first of all have rules that correspond to context formation rules, which by themselves suffice to show reflexivity of
weakening:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\wid \cdot \le \wid \cdot
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\nar \cdot \le \nar \cdot
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid \Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x : \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x : \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid\Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x \div \nar A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid\Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
 {\nar\Gamma'} \le {\nar\Gamma}
\justifies
 ({\nar\Gamma'}, x \div \nar A) \le ({\nar\Gamma}, x \div \nar A)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
 {\nar\Gamma'} \le {\nar\Gamma}
\justifies
 ({\nar\Gamma'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\nar\Gamma}, x :: \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\]

And then furthermore we have rules that capture nontrivial weakening:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid\Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x \div |\wid A|)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
 {\nar\Gamma'} \le {\nar\Gamma}
\justifies
 ({\nar\Gamma'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\nar\Gamma}, x \div |\wid A|)
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid\Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x : \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma'} \le {\wid\Gamma}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma'}, x : \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma}, x \div |\wid A|)
\end{prooftree}
\]

We do a similar thing for substitutions. First the uninteresting rules that look like reflexivity:

\[
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\wid\cdot \le \wid\cdot
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\justifies
\nar\cdot \le \nar\cdot
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta' \wsub{\wid M / x} \le \wid\theta \wsub{\wid M / x}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta'\wsub{\nar M /_{\div} x} \le \wid\theta\wsub{\nar M /_{\div} x}
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta'\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x} \le \wid\theta\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\theta' \le \nar\theta
\justifies
\nar\theta'\nsub{\nar M /_{\div} x} \le \nar\theta\nsub{\nar M /_{\div} x}
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\theta' \le \nar\theta
\justifies
\nar\theta'\nsub{\wid M /_{::} x} \le \nar\theta\nsub{\wid M /_{::} x}
\end{prooftree}
\]
And then the rules that capture nontrivial weakening:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta'\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x} \le \wid\theta\wsub{\erase{\wid M} /_{\div} x}
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\nar\theta' \le \nar\theta
\justifies
\nar\theta'\nsub{\wid M /_{::} x} \le \nar\theta\nsub{\erase{\wid M} /_{\div} x}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta'\wsub{\wid M /_{::} x} \le \wid\theta\wsub{\wid M / x}
\end{prooftree}
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\wid\theta' \le \wid\theta
\justifies
\wid\theta'\wsub{\wid M / x} \le \wid\theta\wsub{\erase{\wid M} /_{\div} x}
\end{prooftree}
\]

\begin{lemma}[Operations are Monotone]
The demotion, promotion, and inclusion operators are monotone with respect to weakening.
\label{lemma.monotonicity.operations}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item If $\wid \Gamma_1 \le \wid \Gamma_2$, then  $\demote{\wid \Gamma_1} \le \demote{\wid \Gamma_2}$.
\item If $\wid \Gamma_1 \le \wid \Gamma_2$, then  $\promote{\wid \Gamma_1} \le \promote{\wid \Gamma_2}$.
\item If $\nar \Gamma_1 \le \nar \Gamma_2$, then  $\ups{\nar \Gamma_1} \le \ups{\nar \Gamma_2}$.
\item If $\wid \theta_1 \le \wid \theta_2$, then  $\demote{\wid \theta_1} \le \demote{\wid \theta_2}$.
\item If $\wid \theta_1 \le \wid \theta_2$, then  $\promote{\wid \theta_1} \le \promote{\wid \theta_2}$.
\item If $\nar \theta_1 \le \nar \theta_2$, then  $\ups{\nar \theta_1} \le \ups{\nar \theta_2}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the derivation of the weakening relation.
The rules that behave the same way on both sides are easy.
An example of a more interesting case:

\begin{itemize}
\item[Case:] Demotion applied to strong-for-ordinary context weakening.
We have
\[
\begin{prooftree}
{\wid\Gamma_1'} \le {\wid\Gamma_2'}
\justifies
({\wid\Gamma_1'}, x :: \wid A) \le ({\wid\Gamma_2'}, x : \wid A)
\end{prooftree}
\]
Our goal is to show
\[\demote{({\wid\Gamma_1'}, x :: \wid A)} \le \demote{({\wid\Gamma_2'}, x : \wid A)}\]
which by the definition of demotion is
\[(\demote{\wid\Gamma_1'}, x :: \wid A) \le (\demote{\wid\Gamma_2'}, x \div \erase{\wid A})\]
By induction hypothesis we have $\demote{\wid\Gamma_1'} \le \demote{\wid\Gamma_2'}$, so we can construct
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Gamma_1'} \le \demote{\wid\Gamma_2'}
\justifies
(\demote{\wid\Gamma_1'}, x :: \wid A) \le (\demote{\wid\Gamma_2'}, x \div \erase{\wid A})
\end{prooftree}
\]
and we are done.
\end{itemize}
\cqed
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Weakening on Variables]
\label{lemma.weakening.variables}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item Suppose $\wid\Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ and
$\wid \Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A$.
Then $\wid \Delta_1 \wprov x : \wid A$.
\item Suppose $\nar\Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ and
$\nar \Delta_2 \nprov x : \nar A$.
Then $\nar \Delta_1 \nprov x : \nar A$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Split cases on the proof that $x$ is well-typed.
\begin{itemize}
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x : \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \wid\Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
$\wid \Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ means that either
$x : \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$
or $x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$.
In both cases,
$\wid\Delta_1 \wprov x : \wid A$ as required.
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \wid\Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
$\wid \Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ means
$x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$, hence
$    \wid\Delta_1 \wprov x : \wid A$ as required.
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x \div \nar A \in  \nar\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \nar\Delta_2 \nprov x : \nar A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
$\nar \Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ means that either
Either $x \div \nar A \in  \nar\Delta_1$
or else $x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_1$ for some $\wid A$ such
that $|\wid A| = \nar A$.
In both cases,
$\nar\Delta_1 \nprov x : \nar A$ as required.
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \nar\Delta_2 \nprov x :  \erase {\wid A}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
$\nar \Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ means
$x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_1$, hence
$    \nar\Delta_1 \nprov x : |\wid A|$ as required.
\end{itemize}
 \cqed
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}[Weakening]
\label{lemma.weakening}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item Suppose $\wid\Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ and
$\wid \Delta_2 \wprov \wid J$.
Then $\wid \Delta_1 \wprov \wid J$.
\item Suppose $\nar\Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ and
$\nar \Delta_2 \nprov \nar J$.
Then $\nar \Delta_1 \nprov \nar J$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the derivation of the judgment being weakened.
The cases for the level-changing constructors follow the premises of their rules, using
Lemma~\ref{lemma.monotonicity.operations}. We give glue type formation as a representative.

\begin{itemize}

\item[Case:] Glue type formation.
\[
\wid\Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2
\qquad
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Delta_2} \nprov \nar A : \nar\rtype \qquad
\promote{\wid\Delta_2}, x \div \nar A \nprov \nar B : \nar\rtype
\justifies
\wid\Delta_2 \wprov \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B : \wid\rtype
\end{prooftree}
\]
Our goal is to show
\[\wid\Delta_1 \wprov \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B : \wid\rtype\]
By Lemma~\ref{lemma.monotonicity.operations}, we have
\[\demote{\wid \Delta_1} \le \demote{\wid \Delta_2}\]
\[\promote{\wid \Delta_1} \le \promote{\wid \Delta_2}\]
and the latter extends by the weakening rule for weak hypotheses to
\[(\promote{\wid \Delta_1}, x \div \nar A) \le (\promote{\wid \Delta_2}, x \div \nar A)\]
Then by induction hypothesis we know
\[\demote{\wid\Delta_1} \nprov \nar A : \nar\rtype\]
\[\promote{\wid\Delta_1}, x \div \nar A \nprov \nar B : \nar\rtype\]
so we can construct
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\demote{\wid\Delta_1} \nprov \nar A : \nar\rtype \qquad
\promote{\wid\Delta_1}, x \div \nar A \nprov \nar B : \nar\rtype
\justifies
\wid\Delta_1 \wprov \gg(x : \nar A).\nar B : \wid\rtype
\end{prooftree}
\]
and we are done.
\end{itemize}
\cqed
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Operations Preserve Typing]
\label{lemma.operations.subst}
Operations applied to substitutions preserve typing in the following sense.
  \begin{itemize}
  \item If $\wprov \wid \Delta \rctx$, then $\nprov \demote{\wid \Delta} \rctx$
  \item If $\wprov \wid \Delta \rctx$, then $\nprov \promote{\wid \Delta} \rctx$
  \item If $\nprov \nar \Delta \rctx$, then $\wprov \ups{\nar \Delta} \rctx$
  \item If $\wid \Delta \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Gamma$, then $\demote{\wid \Delta} \nprov \demote{\wid \theta} : \demote{\wid \Gamma}$.
  \item If $\wid \Delta \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Gamma$, then $\promote{\wid \Delta} \nprov \promote{\wid \theta} : \promote{\wid \Gamma}$.
  \item If $\nar \Delta \nprov \nar \theta : \nar \Gamma$, then $\ups{\nar \Delta} \wprov \ups{\nar \theta} : \ups{\nar \Gamma}$.
  \end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

\cqed
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Indifference on Variables]
\label{lemma.indifference.variables}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta_i : \wid \Delta_i$ and
$\wid\Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ and
$\wid\theta_1 \le \wid\theta_2$ and
$\wid \Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A$,
then $\wid \theta_1(x) = \wid \theta_2(x)$.
\item If $\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar \theta_i : \nar \Delta_i$ and
$\nar\Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ and
$\nar\theta_1 \le \nar\theta_2$ and
$\nar \Delta_2 \nprov x : \nar A$,
then $\nar \theta_1 (x) = \nar \theta_2(x)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Split cases on the proof that $x$ is well-typed.
\begin{itemize}
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x : \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \wid\Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
We know $[\wid M/x] \in \wid \theta_2$ for some $\wid M$.
$\wid \Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ means that either
$x : \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$
or $x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$.
By $\wid \theta_1 \le \wid\theta_2$, either
$[\wid M/x] \in \wid \theta_1$ or
$[\wid M/_{::}x] \in \wid \theta_1$.
In either case,
$\wid \theta_1(x) =  \wid M = \wid \theta_2(x)$ as required.
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \wid\Delta_2 \wprov x : \wid A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
We know $[\wid M/x] \in \wid \theta_2$ for some $\wid M$.
$\wid \Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ means
$x :: \wid A \in  \wid\Delta_1$.
By $\wid \theta_1 \le \wid\theta_2$, we have $[\wid M/x] \in \wid \theta_1$.
Hence $\wid \theta_1(x) = \wid \theta_2(x)$ as required.
\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x \div \nar A \in  \nar\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \nar\Delta_2 \nprov x : \nar A
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
We know $[\nar M/x] \in \nar \theta_2$ for some $\nar M$.
$\nar \Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ means that either
$x \div \nar A \in  \nar\Delta_1$
or $x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_1$ for some $\wid A$
such that $|\wid A| = \nar A$. Let's take these cases one at a time.

If $x : \nar A \in  \nar\Delta_1$, then $\nar \theta_1 \le \nar\theta_2$
means $[\nar M/x] \in \nar\theta_1$, and
$\nar \theta_1(x) =  \nar M = \nar \theta_2(x)$ as required.

Otherwise, $x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_1$. By
$\nar \theta_1 \le \nar\theta_2$ we infer that
$[\wid M/_{::}x] \in \nar\theta_1$ for some $\wid M$ such that
$|\wid M| = \nar M$. Then
$\nar \theta_1(x) =  |\wid M| = \nar M$,
and $\nar \theta_2(x) = \nar M$, as required.

\item[Case:]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_2
    \justifies
    \nar\Delta_2 \nprov x :  \erase {\wid A}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
$\nar \Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ means
$x :: \wid A \in  \nar\Delta_1$, hence
$    \nar\Delta_1 \nprov x : |\wid A|$ as required.
\end{itemize}
  \cqed
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}[Indifference]
\label{lemma.indifference}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item Suppose $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta_i : \wid \Delta_i$ and
$\wid\Delta_1 \le \wid\Delta_2$ and
$\wid\theta_1 \le \wid\theta_2$ and
$\wid \Delta_2 \wprov \wid J$.
Then $\wid \theta_1 \wid J = \wid \theta_2 \wid J$.
\item Suppose $\nar \Gamma \nprov \nar \theta_i : \nar \Delta_i$ and
$\nar\Delta_1 \le \nar\Delta_2$ and
$\nar\theta_1 \le \nar\theta_2$ and
$\nar \Delta_2 \nprov \nar J$.
Then $\nar \theta_1 \nar J = \nar \theta_2 \nar J$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction.
The previous lemma takes care of the variable case.
In all other cases, the two substitutions are pushed into the subexpressions by the
same clause of the definition of substitution, and the induction hypothesis applies to each
subexpression; at the level-crossing subexpressions, the hypotheses of the induction hypothesis are
supplied by Lemma~\ref{lemma.monotonicity.operations} and Lemma~\ref{lemma.operations.subst}.
\cqed
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Weakening Relationship of Operations]\ %
\label{lemma.weakening.operations}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item $\promote{\wid \Gamma} \le \demote{\wid \Gamma} $
  \item $\rpromote{\wid \Gamma} \le \wid \Gamma $
  \item $\wid \Gamma \le \rdemote{\wid \Gamma} $
  \item $\promote{\wid \theta} \le \demote{\wid \theta} $
  \item $\rpromote{\wid \theta} \le \wid \theta $
  \item $\wid \theta \le \rdemote{\wid \theta} $
  \end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

\cqed
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary.indifference}\ %
\begin{itemize}
\item If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Delta$ and
$\demote{\wid\Delta} \nprov \nar M : \nar A$, then
$\demote{\wid\theta} \nar M = \promote{\wid\theta} \nar M$.
\item If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Delta$ and
$\demote{\wid\Delta} \nprov \nar A : \nar \rtype$, then
$\demote{\wid\theta} \nar A = \promote{\wid\theta} \nar A$.
\item
  If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Delta$ and
  $\wid\Delta \wprov \wid M : \wid A$, then
  $\wid\theta\wid M = \rpromote{\wid\theta}\wid M$.
\item
  If $\wid \Gamma \wprov \wid \theta : \wid \Delta$ and
  $\wid\Delta \wprov \wid A : \wid \rtype$, then
  $\wid\theta\wid A = \rpromote{\wid\theta}\wid A$.
\end{itemize}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Use Lemma~\ref{lemma.indifference} and Lemma~\ref{lemma.weakening.operations}.
\cqed
\end{proof}



